<article class="env-definition" id="03R9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03R9">Definition <span data-tag="03R9">59.67.5</span>.</a> A field $K$ is called <em>$C_ r$</em> if for every $0 < d^ r < n$ and every $f \in K[T_1, \ldots , T_ n]$ homogeneous of degree $d$, there exist $\alpha = (\alpha _1, \ldots , \alpha _ n)$, $\alpha _ i \in K$ not all zero, such that $f(\alpha ) = 0$. Such an $\alpha $ is called a <em>nontrivial solution</em> of $f$. </p>
</article>